<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="ex-ga-vector-potential-full" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Vector Potential &amp; Gauge Transformations in Geometric Algebra</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>Setup:</alert> Consider a uniform magnetic field <m>\vec{B} = B_0 \mathbf{e}_z</m>.
            In Geometric Algebra (GA), we represent this as the bivector:
            <md>
                \bivec{B} = B_0 (\mathbf{e}_x \wedge \mathbf{e}_y) \, .
            </md>
            Note that in 3D, the traditional vector <m>\vec{B}</m> is the dual of this bivector via <m>\vec{B} = -I \bivec{B}</m>, where <m>I = \mathbf{e}_x \mathbf{e}_y \mathbf{e}_z</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that vector potentials <m>\vec{A}</m> for symmetry gauge and Landau gauge satisfy the
                condition that <m>\bivec{B} = \vec{\nabla} \wedge \vec{A}</m> for a uniform magnetic field.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <ul>
                <li><term>Landau Gauge:</term> <md>\vec{A}_{\text{Landau}} = -B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x</md></li>
                <li><term>Symmetry Gauge:</term> <md>\vec{A}_{\text{sym}} = \frac{B_0}{2}(x\mathbf{e}_y - y\mathbf{e}_x)</md></li>
            </ul>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
            Part 1: Show that the Landau gauge potential satisfies <m>\bivec{B}=\vec{\nabla}\wedge\vec{A}</m> such that
            <m>\bivec{B} = B_0\mathbf{e}_x\mathbf{e}_y</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <em>Step 1:</em> Rewrite the Landau gauge potential in vector form.
                <md>
                    \vec{A}_{\text{Landau}} = -B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x = \begin{bmatrix} -B_0 y \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <em>Step 2:</em> Evaluate <m>\bivec{B} = \vec{\nabla} \wedge \vec{A}_{\text{Landau}}</m>.
                <md>
                    <mrow>
                        \vec{\nabla} \wedge \vec{A}_{\text{Landau}} \amp=
                        \begin{bmatrix} \partial_x \\ \partial_y \\ \partial_z \end{bmatrix}
                        \wedge
                        \begin{bmatrix} -B_0 y \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp=
                        (\partial_x \mathbf{e}_x) \wedge (-B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x + 0\mathbf{e}_y + 0\mathbf{e}_z)
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp\quad + (\partial_y \mathbf{e}_y) \wedge (-B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x + 0\mathbf{e}_y + 0\mathbf{e}_z)
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp\quad + (\partial_z \mathbf{e}_z) \wedge (-B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x + 0\mathbf{e}_y + 0\mathbf{e}_z)
                    </mrow>

                    <mrow>
                        \amp=
                        (\partial_x \mathbf{e}_x) \wedge (-B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x)
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp\quad + (\partial_y \mathbf{e}_y) \wedge (-B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x)
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp\quad + (\partial_z \mathbf{e}_z) \wedge (-B_0 y \mathbf{e}_x)
                    </mrow>

                    <mrow>
                        \amp=
                        \cancelto{0}{(-\partial_x B_0 y)}\cancelto{0}{(\mathbf{e}_x \wedge \mathbf{e}_x)}
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp\quad + (-\partial_y B_0 y)(\mathbf{e}_y \wedge \mathbf{e}_x)
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp\quad + \cancelto{0}{(-\partial_z B_0 y)}(\mathbf{e}_z \wedge \mathbf{e}_x)
                    </mrow>

                    <mrow>
                        \amp= -B_0(-\mathbf{e}_x \wedge \mathbf{e}_y)
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        \amp= B_0 (\mathbf{e}_x \wedge \mathbf{e}_y) \quad \proofmark
                    </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

        </solution>
        <solution>
            <p>Part 2: Show that the symmetry gauge potential satisfies <m>\bivec{B}=\vec{\nabla}\wedge\vec{A}</m>.</p>

        </solution>

    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Define a gauge transformation as <m>\vec{A}' = \vec{A} + \vec{\nabla}\chi</m>.
                What is the physical significance of the scalar function <m>\chi</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the specific scalar function <m>\chi(x,y)</m> that transforms the Landau gauge potential into the Symmetry gauge potential:
                <md>
                    \vec{A}_{\text{sym}} = \vec{A}_{\text{Landau}} + \vec{\nabla}\chi
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            Calculate the difference <m>\vec{A}_{\text{sym}} - \vec{A}_{\text{Landau}}</m> and integrate component-wise.
        </hint>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Prove that the magnetic bivector is invariant under this transformation:
                <md>
                    \bivec{B}' = \vec{\nabla} \wedge \vec{A}' = \bivec{B}
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Why does adding the gradient term <m>\vec{\nabla}\chi</m> not change the bivector field <m>\bivec{B}</m>?
                Explain this in terms of the properties of the wedge product and second derivatives.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>